WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:01.550
This is a shot

00:00:01.550 --> 00:00:04.540
from Pixar's latest movie, "Soul."

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:06.700
Even though it's
animated, everything about

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:09.630
how the fingers float
from one key to another

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:12.890
to the small details like
tendons moving in the hand

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:14.330
feels just as real as

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:16.920
the reference footage that inspired it.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.770
While Pixar has used reference
footage since the beginning,

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:21.350
it could not have pulled off a scene

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:23.660
with movement this nuanced and realistic

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:26.780
back in 1995 for "Toy Story."

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:29.900
And it took years of
technological breakthroughs.

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:32.313
So how did Pixar get here?

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:37.480
First off, they have an army
of animators and riggers.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:39.680
We talked to seven who worked on "Soul"

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:41.970
to see how far Pixar has come.

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:44.680
Let's start over 26 years ago

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:47.760
with the technology built for "Toy Story."

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.450
Getting a given character to move

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:52.730
starts in Pixar's rigging
and modeling department.

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:54.330
Whenever you're thinking
of a character model

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:57.070
before it's rigged, it's in
a completely neutral pose.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:00:58.470
There's no expression on the face.

00:00:58.470 --> 00:00:59.970
The arms and legs are all straight.

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:02.280
And so the controls that
we put in in rigging

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.960
lets parts of the body
move like you would pose

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:06.630
like a doll or a puppet.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:08.870
To get a clearer idea of the basics,

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:11.460
let's start with this
character model of Woody.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.400
The rigging department will add the rig,

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.780
which is essentially an inner skeleton.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.380
Throughout this rig you
can see all these controls

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:21.260
called animated variables, or avars,

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:22.630
which animators use to make

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:24.670
different parts of a
character move around.

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:28.690
This early version of Woody
had 596 of those controls,

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:30.160
which may seem like a lot,

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:35.150
but by 2019's "Toy Story 4,"
Woody had more than 7,000.

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:37.170
To make things a little more complicated,

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:39.140
one control point is often connected

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:40.720
to other control points.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:42.360
So moving one in the eyebrow

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:45.010
will move the wrinkles on the forehead.

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:47.590
Adding more controls to
these "Toy Story" characters

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:49.730
would have let them move with more nuance,

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:52.840
but back then, Pixar was
making a brand new rig

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.140
for every single character.

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:57.930
That would change with "Toy Story 2."

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:01.780
Pixar introduced Geppetto,
later updated to Presto,

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:03.570
which allowed animators to reuse

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:06.040
and adapt rigs for multiple characters.

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:07.830
This sped up the animation process

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:09.500
and gave animators more control

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:12.000
over movements and facial expressions.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.530
The new software provided
a template of two-legged

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:16.510
and four-legged rigs that could be altered

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:19.320
depending on the specific
needs of a character.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:21.940
According to rigger
Jared Fong, the sea lions

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:25.630
in "Finding Dory" were actually
constructed from dog rigs,

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:27.790
with legs folded into flippers.

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:29.970
More control with Geppetto also meant

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:32.320
more expressive facial movements,

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:34.820
as seen in 2003's "Finding Nemo."

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:37.330
To really nail the character's emotions,

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:39.880
animators used reference footage.

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:42.280
Now fish don't have eyebrows or eyelids,

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:46.030
but eyebrows are so important
for expressing human emotions.

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:48.960
So animators decided to
add eyebrow-like lumps

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.270
to characters like Marlin and Dory.

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:53.360
And replicating real facial expressions

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:55.920
meant connecting more control
points with each other.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.020
You can see that with
Dory. The control points

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:00.810
around her mouth are connected
with her eyes and her cheeks,

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:03.230
giving her a more believable smile.

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:04.830
A smize, if you will.

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:08.070
After making major strides
with toys and sea creatures,

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:12.030
the most daunting challenge
was yet to come: humans.

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:16.500
Mr. Incredible consisted
of 426 primary controls,

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:20.710
111 secondary animation controls
for more subtle movements,

00:03:20.710 --> 00:03:23.510
and 1,061 modeling controls

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:25.800
for sizing and other adjustments.

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.040
Thanks to Geppetto, the
rigging and modeling team

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:29.600
could perfect Mr. Incredible

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:32.470
and then reuse his rig
for other characters.

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:34.520
And to really make his movements realistic

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:36.660
they used full-body reference footage.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:38.930
For example, this clip where an animator

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:40.830
lifts up this exercise ball

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:42.550
was helpful for moments like this,

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:45.280
where Mr. Incredible
lifts up an Omnidroid.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:48.750
And the team didn't animate
just a rigid skeleton.

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:51.380
On top of the skeleton
was a layer of muscles,

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:53.230
which could bulge and flex.

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:54.830
And the skin on top of that

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:57.060
would need to respond realistically.

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:00.340
So Pixar developed a
new system called Goo.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:02.500
This allowed the animators to see the skin

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:05.000
and muscles react in
real time as they worked,

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:07.110
allowing for greater
flexibility and movement

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:10.060
and more control over how humans move.

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:11.670
Goo led to breakthroughs in moving

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:14.370
more challenging body
parts, like the shoulders,

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:17.070
which were more primitive
in earlier films.

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:19.620
The Buzz Lightyear
shoulder's a ball and socket.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:21.560
Woody's shoulder is just a stitch line.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:22.710
He's got no shoulders, right?

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:24.120
That arm just hinges.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:28.080
Mr. Incredible has a
big old strong shoulder.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.170
How you get a trapezius
muscle there in the shoulder,

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:33.330
and how it moves in the
pectoral, really complex stuff.

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:34.900
The rigs built for "The Incredibles"

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:36.220
were designed to let the characters

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:38.540
stretch further than the average human.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:40.560
Which was important for Elastigirl,

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.880
who needed to transform
into all shapes and sizes.

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:45.440
And just like how superheroes

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:47.860
needed to react to forces like gravity,

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:52.360
so too did the cars in
Pixar's next feature, "Cars."

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:55.320
Pixar extended its technological
boundaries yet again

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:57.560
with a system called ground locking,

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:00.720
which made sure the cars
always stayed on the same path,

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:01.830
regardless of terrain,

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:05.200
without animators having to
move them frame by frame.

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:06.480
We had to have our characters

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.540
be able to follow the
contours of the ground

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:10.940
without having to animate them,

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:13.820
like up and down if it
was like, a rolling hill.

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:15.830
So it's going to move along these things.

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:17.780
And then we're going to
animate on top of that.

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:19.010
Because of ground locking,

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:21.980
Lightning McQueen could now
drive smoothly on a flat road

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:25.490
that turns into a very
windy path automatically.

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:27.480
This may seem like a specific advancement

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:30.110
just for the "Cars" movies,
but it would be important

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:33.280
for some of the most memorable
characters in "Soul."

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:34.798
By 2007, animators could have

00:05:34.798 --> 00:05:38.390
up to 150 controls in the human face.

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:41.960
So they could give characters
a much wider range of emotion.

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:44.040
Perfect for a movie like "Ratatouille,"

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:46.420
which is largely set in the human world.

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:48.310
All these intricate controls allowed for

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:51.910
the complex array of expressions
seen on Chef Skinner.

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:54.720
Look at how far Chef
Skinner's mouth extends

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:56.320
as he's yelling at Linguini.

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:58.780
According to supervising
animator Mark Walsh,

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:01.250
the tech wouldn't have been
able to handle such a stretch

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:03.490
in "Toy Story" or "Finding Nemo."

00:06:03.490 --> 00:06:05.770
The groundbreaking muscle
work in "The Incredibles"

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:07.640
allowed animators to give Linguini

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:09.970
more spontaneous movements in the scenes

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:12.960
where he's controlled
by Remy in the kitchen.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.463
By the time "Toy Story
3" came out in 2010,

00:06:15.463 --> 00:06:18.690
Pixar's animators had
gotten such precise control

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:20.860
over how characters move,
that it's surprising

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:22.810
they'd want to take a step backward.

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:25.650
But they actually relied on fewer controls

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:28.070
to better replicate the
trademark toy movements

00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:29.870
audiences knew and loved.

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:31.650
If a character has a
limited range of motion

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:34.170
in its shoulder, what
we'll do is we'll basically

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:35.780
lock off some of that range of motion,

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:38.810
and then that forces you to
animate it in a different way.

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:40.290
They still had room to pick and choose

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:42.220
what they would keep
about the old characters

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:44.350
and where to elevate them.

00:06:44.350 --> 00:06:46.850
For example, they could
have put 25 controls

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:50.600
in each of Buzz's eyebrows, but
decided to keep them simple,

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:53.190
like when he had just three
per brow in the original,

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:54.940
while also giving Spanish Buzz

00:06:54.940 --> 00:06:57.740
these complicated flamenco-like moves.

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:01.280
There was one particularly
hard toy to animate:

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:03.730
the octopus named Stretch.

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:05.530
Despite the innovations in stretchiness

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:08.040
from "The Incredibles,"
making eight tentacles

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:11.590
stretch and curl was
still a daunting task.

00:07:11.590 --> 00:07:12.423
We're fighting against the computer.

00:07:12.423 --> 00:07:14.160
The computer wants to make things

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:16.470
that are that are solid
and that are perfect.

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:17.930
But it was a challenge they would

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:20.860
eventually master in "Finding Dory."

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:22.240
The hardest part of an octopus

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:24.000
to replicate is the tentacles,

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:27.000
which need to bend, curl, and unfurl.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:29.290
You can see early attempts
at this with Stretch

00:07:29.290 --> 00:07:30.850
and this octopus-like monster

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:32.780
all the way back in "Monsters, Inc."

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:34.790
In "Finding Dory," Hank also needed

00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:37.510
to move around quite a
bit and grab onto things,

00:07:37.510 --> 00:07:39.070
like this coffeepot.

00:07:39.070 --> 00:07:42.660
Hank also had more than
350 suckers combined

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:43.860
on his tentacles.

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:47.460
Those were so unpredictable
that they had to be simulated,

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:50.380
a process where movements
are automated using code,

00:07:50.380 --> 00:07:53.270
like with strands of hair and clothing.

00:07:53.270 --> 00:07:56.500
The tentacles themselves were
so dynamic that when one moved

00:07:56.500 --> 00:07:59.210
it would move other parts
of Hank's body as well.

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:02.170
And animators used an altered
version of this tentacle rig

00:08:02.170 --> 00:08:06.890
on Dante in "Coco," giving
the dog a long, twisty tongue.

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:08.380
You can see it grab onto objects

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:10.980
here in the short film "Dante's Lunch."

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:14.830
But the biggest challenge
for "Coco" was music.

00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:16.700
Pixar had to figure out how to get Miguel

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:18.550
to accurately play guitar.

00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:21.120
So they strapped GoPros
directly to guitars,

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:24.090
giving themselves the
closest possible angle.

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:25.570
They would listen to the guitar music

00:08:25.570 --> 00:08:28.560
and mimic the exact finger
and string movements.

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:30.540
It looks so authentic.

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:34.080
But Pixar found it could
push this even further

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:35.210
and make playing music

00:08:35.210 --> 00:08:39.160
look even more real in 2020's "Soul."

00:08:39.160 --> 00:08:40.830
As with previous Pixar films,

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:43.130
some assets were reused from "Coco."

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:45.990
For example, the same rig
built for the "Coco" guitars

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:48.230
was repurposed for Miho's bass.

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:50.280
We modified it for four strings

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:52.600
and just kind of repositioned
it to work for that.

00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:54.680
For the scenes where Joe plays piano,

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:57.030
they went far beyond
just matching the music

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:58.240
directly to audio.

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:01.170
For reference footage,
Pixar captured Jon Batiste

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:03.510
playing piano at multiple angles.

00:09:03.510 --> 00:09:06.150
Then, character technical
director Jason Davies

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:08.580
and the rigging team built a piano rig,

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:10.600
which functions like a digital piano.

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:13.410
Davies fed the audio files
directly into the rig,

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:15.300
and the piano would play automatically

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:16.640
in time with the music.

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:19.250
If a key lit up blue,
the animators would place

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:21.690
Joe's left hand there, and his right hand

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:24.210
would be placed on any
key that turned red.

00:09:24.210 --> 00:09:26.410
They also tried developing a simulation

00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:28.950
for Joe's piano playing,
but it proved tricky,

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:32.380
as his finger was too heavy
on the small piano keys.

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:34.690
So Davies developed a finger contact rig

00:09:34.690 --> 00:09:37.170
similar to one used on "Coco."

00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:38.760
Just like in "Coco," where fingers

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:40.600
moved the strings on guitars,

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:42.950
Dorothea's fingers could
play the saxophone,

00:09:42.950 --> 00:09:46.040
and most importantly,
Joe could play the piano.

00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:49.170
When their fingers would
intersect the space

00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:52.180
where a key was, so it pushes
down into the keyboard,

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:54.760
it would calculate the proper angle

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:56.760
that that piano key needed to rotate

00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:58.660
to make it look like he's pushing it.

00:09:58.660 --> 00:10:00.360
While the piano played on its own,

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:02.090
the hand still needed to capture

00:10:02.090 --> 00:10:04.180
the complicated motions of playing.

00:10:04.180 --> 00:10:07.390
Which is why Joe was
equipped with 292 controls

00:10:07.390 --> 00:10:08.870
in each of his hands.

00:10:08.870 --> 00:10:10.700
That's about half the amount of controls

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:13.750
in Woody's entire body
in the first "Toy Story."

00:10:13.750 --> 00:10:16.010
The riggers added variables to Joe's hand

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:18.140
that they had never used before.

00:10:18.140 --> 00:10:20.820
According to character
model lead Mara MacMahon,

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:22.740
the team even added a control

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:25.187
for each tendon in Joe's hands.

00:10:25.187 --> 00:10:26.220
So that was something that could kind of

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:28.360
capture the tension in his
hands as he's playing the piano.

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:29.880
So there's a control for each hand,

00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:32.160
just to make a tendon
appear if the animator

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:35.510
wanted to really, like,
emphasize that particular pose.

00:10:35.510 --> 00:10:37.850
These state-of-the-art
hand and wrist controls

00:10:37.850 --> 00:10:39.880
weren't just useful for playing music.

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:42.110
They also allowed for flexibility.

00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:43.950
Like Linguini in "Ratatouille,"

00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:47.680
Joe is tall and lanky and has
a lot of frenzied movements

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:49.300
that had to be highlighted in scenes

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:51.450
where he is inhabited by 22.

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:55.970
For all of this, Joe had
477 controls in his face

00:10:55.970 --> 00:10:59.970
and 789 additional controls
throughout his entire body,

00:10:59.970 --> 00:11:04.180
giving him 1,266 controls total.

00:11:04.180 --> 00:11:06.820
Conversely, they limited
the movements on Dorothea

00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:08.830
to show her regal stature.

00:11:08.830 --> 00:11:11.865
They even went as far as
removing subtle blinks and nods.

00:11:11.865 --> 00:11:14.412
It's all in like, the look, you know.

00:11:14.412 --> 00:11:16.884
How they hold their head
up, or how they just

00:11:16.884 --> 00:11:18.547
set their gaze upon
whatever they're looking at.

00:11:18.547 --> 00:11:21.630
But sometimes it's the
simplest-looking actions

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:24.800
or characters that prove
the hardest to control.

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:25.900
Take the counselors.

00:11:25.900 --> 00:11:28.200
They look like simple 2D lines,

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:31.630
but they were the most
complicated characters to animate.

00:11:31.630 --> 00:11:33.370
Doing the counselor characters for "Soul"

00:11:33.370 --> 00:11:35.810
was pushing our limits
of what we could do.

00:11:35.810 --> 00:11:37.370
And they turned out amazing.

00:11:37.370 --> 00:11:41.780
That's building on 25, 26 years of history

00:11:41.780 --> 00:11:43.270
to get to that point.

00:11:43.270 --> 00:11:46.600
Terry consisted of around 300 controls.

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:48.610
A new tension control allowed animators

00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:52.300
to sharpen or add an angle by
assigning it a higher value.

00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:53.890
This allowed Terry and the Jerrys

00:11:53.890 --> 00:11:56.900
to randomly grow fingers off their hands.

00:11:56.900 --> 00:11:59.230
Plus, they were able to
easily transition between

00:11:59.230 --> 00:12:02.300
Terry's multiple forms
using just a few controls,

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:05.790
pivotal in scenes where Terry
mutates into objects on Earth,

00:12:05.790 --> 00:12:08.260
like this handrail, or this bag of chips.

00:12:08.260 --> 00:12:10.860
But of course, even
something so new and unusual

00:12:10.860 --> 00:12:13.860
couldn't have been made without
looking to Pixar's past.

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:16.900
The stretchy software that
built Hank and Dante's tongue

00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:19.890
allowed Terry to disguise
herself in elaborate ways,

00:12:19.890 --> 00:12:22.800
like in this heart monitor,
and this brick wall.

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:24.290
And the counselors couldn't just

00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:26.130
move the way a person could,

00:12:26.130 --> 00:12:28.330
posing another major challenge.

00:12:28.330 --> 00:12:29.740
Since they don't have legs, right,

00:12:29.740 --> 00:12:31.300
They just slide around on the ground.

00:12:31.300 --> 00:12:33.090
The closest analogy is actually "Cars"

00:12:33.090 --> 00:12:34.070
and that kind of stuff.

00:12:34.070 --> 00:12:35.440
That meant they needed to borrow from

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:38.760
the same ground-locking
technology pioneered in "Cars"

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:40.810
to keep the counselors in motion.

00:12:40.810 --> 00:12:44.050
When you get so good at making
things move realistically,

00:12:44.050 --> 00:12:47.560
it's OK to sometimes let
your imagination run wild.

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:49.160
We're not trying to make things look

00:12:49.160 --> 00:12:51.040
necessarily perfectly real.

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:54.120
What we're trying to do is
make sure that the emotions

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:55.360
that are coming out of the characters,

00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:59.277
and what they're thinking, feel 100% real.

